  <h2 id="india-demand">India demand: what the numbers actually say</h2>

  <p>
    Global reports matter, but the India picture is specific enough to be worth reading separately.
    Here is what current data says about where real demand is forming in India by 2030.
  </p>

  <div class="grid-two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Technology and AI</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>India's AI market is projected to reach $17 billion by 2027, growing 25–35% annually.</li>
        <li>NASSCOM reports AI roles growing 40%+ year on year, with a 51% talent gap — meaning demand far outpaces available people. See the <a href="/blog/ai-future/artificial-intelligence-career-paths/">real AI career path map</a> for where those roles actually sit.</li>
        <li>India's public cloud market is on track to hit $17.8 billion by 2027 at 23% annual growth, creating over 1.4 crore new cloud-linked jobs.</li>
        <li>WEF's Future of Jobs 2025 report projects that 39% of workers will need significant reskilling between 2025 and 2030.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Clean energy and physical infrastructure</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>India's National Solar Mission targets 500 GW of renewable energy capacity by 2030 — a massive build-out with near-zero trained workforce today.</li>
        <li>India's EV market is projected to reach ₹50,000 crore by 2030, with battery technology, motor control, and EV software integration as the highest-demand specialisations.</li>
        <li>India needs 30 million or more skilled workers by 2030 across technology, health, and clean energy sectors.</li>
        <li>The global energy workforce reached 76 million in 2024, with the IEA flagging major shortfalls in technical roles.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    These numbers point to one thing: the skill gap in India is large enough that a student who builds real capability in any of these fields — not just awareness — is entering a market where demand currently outpaces supply.
  </p>

